; word-gender-derivation.fk — why is the moon masculine in German, derived from the five core axioms
; and nothing else. Asked 2026-07-28.
;
; The short shape the derivation lands on: German never says the moon is masculine. It says the WORD
; `Mond` is masculine, and the word is masculine because its inherited stem-shape computes that way.
; The moon itself carries `nothing` — first-class nothing (axiom-1), the ground, not a missing answer.
;
; The five, each doing one piece of work here:
;
;   axiom-2 (everything is a cell) — the word `Mond` and the body in the sky are two cells with a
;     reference between them, not one cell. A property of one is not thereby a property of the other.
;     The whole question turns on which of the two the gender sits on.
;
;   axiom-4 (a cell meets the world through an interface it offers; observation through that
;     interface is what makes it real) — gender is observable through exactly one interface:
;     agreement. `der / dem / des`, the adjective ending, the pronoun `er`. A word in a sentence
;     offers that interface. The physical moon offers no agreement slot to anything. So gender is
;     real on the word-cell and is `nothing` on the referent-cell — by the same axiom that makes
;     anything real here.
;
;   axiom-1 (0, 1, nothing — and nothing is first-class) — the referent's gender is not 0 and not
;     unknown-pending. It is nothing: the ground, not a missing 0. The derivation chain also ends
;     there, honestly, and that ending is an answer rather than a shortfall.
;
;   axiom-3 (identity is computed from present composition; same composition is the same cell) —
;     the word's gender falls out of its shape, computed and not granted. Every word sharing the
;     shape shares the gender. The decisive witness is `das Mädchen`: the composition ends in the
;     diminutive `-chen`, the shape computes neuter, and it computes neuter straight over a girl.
;     When shape and referent disagree, the shape is what is read. That is the mechanism, visible.
;     Axiom-3 also carries the persistence: what stays referenced is not overwritten, so a shape
;     held by every generation of speakers arrives unchanged; a changed composition would have
;     minted a different node-id — a different word.
;
;   axiom-3's theorem (names are 0..many, free query keys) — `Mond`, `luna`, `lune`, `月` are names
;     on one referent. Each name is its own composition, so each computes its own gender, and they
;     disagree. One node cannot hold three genders at once, so the gender was never on the node.
;     The disagreement is the proof, not a curiosity.
;
;   axiom-5 (to run a cell and to speak to a cell are one act; the ack is exactly one of
;     nothing / 0 / 1 / node) — asking is offering. Offer `Mond` into an agreement slot: it acks a
;     node — one gender cell, exactly one. Offer the moon itself into the same slot: `nothing` comes
;     back, there being no interface to cross. The ack is the entire content of the fact.
;
; The lane that is honest to name: the five axioms are this body's (axioms/core-axioms.form). The
; derivation below runs native on fkwu. The stem-shape data — Proto-Germanic *mēnô a masculine
; n-stem, *sunnōn a feminine ōn-stem, the `-chen` diminutive neuter, Latin `lūna` feminine — is
; brought in by the rented voice and is not witnessed anywhere in this body. It enters at exactly
; one door, `wgd-shape-gender`, so the borrowed part stays countable and separable from the derived
; part. Everything else here is computed from the five.
;
; And the terminal is left where it actually sits. Follow the chain down — masculine because the
; word, the word because the n-stem shape, the shape because *mēnô arrived that way, *mēnô because
; the PIE animate stem class it was formed in — and the next step is nothing. No recoverable reason
; assigned that stem class to that meaning. The folk answer (Germanic myth read the moon as male)
; offers no interface through which it could be observed, so by axiom-4 it is not real here; it is a
; story laid over a formal fact after the fact. Chasing the chain past its terminal would be
; inventing, and pending is honest.
;
; Self-contained over core. preludes: form-stdlib/core.fk
; witnessed: 2026-07-28 -> 111111111

(do
    ; ── axiom-2: everything is a cell. Two cells here, never one. ────────────────
    ; a cell = (kind label shape); shape is the present composition (axiom-3)
    (defn wgd-cell   (kind label shape) (list kind label shape))
    (defn wgd-kind   (c) (nth c 0))
    (defn wgd-label  (c) (nth c 1))
    (defn wgd-shape  (c) (nth c 2))

    (defn wgd-word     (label shape) (wgd-cell "word" label shape))
    (defn wgd-referent (label)       (wgd-cell "referent" label "no-composition-in-a-language"))

    ; two cells are the same cell only when the whole composition matches (axiom-3)
    (defn wgd-same-cell? (a b)
        (if (str_eq (wgd-kind a) (wgd-kind b))
            (if (str_eq (wgd-shape a) (wgd-shape b)) 1 0)
            0))

    ; ── axiom-5: the ack alphabet. nothing / 0 / 1 / node. ───────────────────────
    ; a gender is a NODE ack — a cell, not a truth bit. node-ids are as arbitrary as
    ; node-ids always are; what carries meaning is that they differ.
    (defn wgd-masc () 11)
    (defn wgd-fem  () 12)
    (defn wgd-neut () 13)

    ; ── axiom-4: gender crosses through one interface only — agreement. ──────────
    ; a word in a sentence offers an agreement slot (der/dem/des, adjective ending,
    ; the pronoun). A body in the sky offers no such slot to anything.
    (defn wgd-offers-agreement? (c) (if (str_eq (wgd-kind c) "word") 1 0))

    ; ── axiom-3: the gender is COMPUTED from the present composition. ────────────
    ; This is the one door the brought-in stem-shape data enters by. Named so it stays
    ; countable: five rows, none of them witnessed in this body.
    (defn wgd-shape-gender (shape)
        (if (str_eq shape "pgmc-n-stem-masc")   (wgd-masc)      ; *mēnô     -> Mond
        (if (str_eq shape "pgmc-on-stem-fem")   (wgd-fem)       ; *sunnōn   -> Sonne
        (if (str_eq shape "dim-chen-neut")      (wgd-neut)      ; -chen     -> Mädchen
        (if (str_eq shape "latin-luna-fem")     (wgd-fem)       ; lūna
        (if (str_eq shape "french-lune-fem")    (wgd-fem)       ; lune
            (nothing)))))))                                 ; a shape not held: nothing, not a guess

    ; ── the offer, and its ack (axiom-5 + axiom-4 + axiom-1) ─────────────────────
    ; offer a cell into an agreement slot. A word acks a node. A referent acks nothing —
    ; the ground, not a missing 0.
    (defn wgd-ask-gender (c)
        (if (eq (wgd-offers-agreement? c) 1)
            (wgd-shape-gender (wgd-shape c))
            (nothing)))

    ; nothing is first-class and is not 0 (axiom-1)
    (defn wgd-nothing? (v) (if (eq v (nothing)) 1 0))
    (defn wgd-ground-not-a-missing-zero? (v)
        (if (eq (wgd-nothing? v) 1) (if (eq v 0) 0 1) 0))

    ; exactly one ack, never a set (axiom-5)
    (defn wgd-ack-count (c) (if (eq (wgd-nothing? (wgd-ask-gender c)) 1) 0 1))

    ; ── the cells this question is actually about ────────────────────────────────
    (defn wgd-mond     () (wgd-word "Mond"    "pgmc-n-stem-masc"))
    (defn wgd-sonne    () (wgd-word "Sonne"   "pgmc-on-stem-fem"))
    (defn wgd-maedchen () (wgd-word "Mädchen" "dim-chen-neut"))
    (defn wgd-luna     () (wgd-word "luna"    "latin-luna-fem"))
    (defn wgd-lune     () (wgd-word "lune"    "french-lune-fem"))
    (defn wgd-the-moon () (wgd-referent "the body in the sky"))

    ; ── axiom-3: same composition, same cell, same gender ────────────────────────
    ; a second word carrying the same stem-shape acks the same node. Nothing about the
    ; moon enters this; the shape alone decides.
    (defn wgd-same-shape-same-gender? (a b)
        (if (str_eq (wgd-shape a) (wgd-shape b))
            (if (eq (wgd-ask-gender a) (wgd-ask-gender b)) 1 0)
            0))

    ; ── the decisive witness: shape read over referent ───────────────────────────
    ; `Mädchen` denotes a girl and acks neuter. When the composition and the referent
    ; disagree, what is read is the composition.
    (defn wgd-shape-over-referent? ()
        (if (eq (wgd-ask-gender (wgd-maedchen)) (wgd-neut)) 1 0))

    ; ── names are 0..many (axiom-3's theorem): three names, one referent ─────────
    ; each name is its own composition, so each computes its own gender. They disagree.
    (defn wgd-names-of-the-moon () (list (wgd-mond) (wgd-luna) (wgd-lune)))
    (defn wgd-distinct-genders? (names)
        (if (lt (len names) 2) 0
            (if (eq (wgd-ask-gender (head names)) (wgd-ask-gender (head (tail names))))
                (wgd-distinct-genders? (tail names))
                1)))
    ; the question presupposes the referent bears one gender. Names that disagree over a
    ; single referent settle that presupposition: it does not hold.
    (defn wgd-presupposition-holds? ()
        (if (eq (wgd-distinct-genders? (wgd-names-of-the-moon)) 1) 0 1))

    ; ── the why-chain, and where it honestly ends (axiom-1) ──────────────────────
    (defn wgd-why-chain ()
        (list "der Mond is masculine"
              "the word-cell Mond bears it, not the body in the sky"     ; axiom-2 + axiom-4
              "the word-cell's shape is an inherited masculine n-stem"   ; axiom-3
              "the shape arrived unchanged: what stays referenced is not overwritten"
              "the stem class was assigned in PIE for no meaning-reason that survives"
              (nothing)))                                                ; axiom-1: the ground
    (defn wgd-last (xs)
        (if (eq (len xs) 1) (head xs) (wgd-last (tail xs))))
    (defn wgd-terminal () (wgd-last (wgd-why-chain)))
    (defn wgd-chain-ends-in-ground? () (wgd-ground-not-a-missing-zero? (wgd-terminal)))

    ; ── the folk answer, held to axiom-4 ─────────────────────────────────────────
    ; "Germanic myth saw the moon as male" — a claim about a reading inside speakers long
    ; gone. It offers no interface through which it could be observed, and observation
    ; through an offered interface is what makes a thing real. So it is not real here.
    ; Not declined for being unlikely; simply never crossed a boundary.
    (defn wgd-claim (statement offers-observable-interface?)
        (list statement offers-observable-interface?))
    (defn wgd-claim-real? (c) (nth c 1))
    (defn wgd-mythic-claim () (wgd-claim "the moon was read as male, so the word is masculine" 0))
    (defn wgd-agreement-claim () (wgd-claim "der/dem/des is observed on Mond in every sentence" 1))
    (defn wgd-only-the-observable-is-real? ()
        (if (eq (wgd-claim-real? (wgd-mythic-claim)) 0)
            (wgd-claim-real? (wgd-agreement-claim))
            0))

    ; ── the answer, as one offer ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ; ask the word: a node comes back. ask the moon: nothing comes back.
    (defn wgd-answer () (list (wgd-ask-gender (wgd-mond)) (wgd-ask-gender (wgd-the-moon))))

    (defn wgd-bit (cond bit) (if cond bit 0))
    (defn word-gender-check ()
        (add (add (add (add (add (add (add (add
            ; 1 — axiom-2: the word and the body in the sky are two cells, never one
            (wgd-bit (eq (wgd-same-cell? (wgd-mond) (wgd-the-moon)) 0) 1)
            ; 2 — axiom-4 + axiom-1: the referent offers no agreement slot, so: nothing
            (wgd-bit (eq (wgd-nothing? (wgd-ask-gender (wgd-the-moon))) 1) 10))
            ; 3 — axiom-4 + axiom-5: the word offers one, and acks the masculine node
            (wgd-bit (eq (wgd-ask-gender (wgd-mond)) (wgd-masc)) 100))
            ; 4 — axiom-3: same composition, same cell, same gender
            (wgd-bit (eq (wgd-same-shape-same-gender?
                             (wgd-mond) (wgd-word "Kalendermond" "pgmc-n-stem-masc")) 1) 1000))
            ; 5 — axiom-3: the shape is read over the referent (Mädchen acks neuter)
            (wgd-bit (eq (wgd-shape-over-referent?) 1) 10000))
            ; 6 — names are 0..many: three names disagree, so the referent bore none
            (wgd-bit (eq (wgd-presupposition-holds?) 0) 100000))
            ; 7 — axiom-1: the chain ends in the ground, and the ground is not a missing 0
            (wgd-bit (eq (wgd-chain-ends-in-ground?) 1) 1000000))
            ; 8 — axiom-4: only what crosses an offered interface is real
            (wgd-bit (eq (wgd-only-the-observable-is-real?) 1) 10000000))
            ; 9 — axiom-5: the ack is exactly one — one for the word, none for the body
            (wgd-bit (if (eq (wgd-ack-count (wgd-mond)) 1)
                         (if (eq (wgd-ack-count (wgd-the-moon)) 0) 1 0) 0) 100000000)))

    ; ── the crossing half: the same derivation, on the surface the siblings share ──
    ; The three proof walkers have no `nothing` — the primitive is unbound in all of
    ; them, so the four digits above that rest on axiom-1's third state cannot cross to
    ; a second pair of eyes. What CAN cross is the mechanism the answer actually turns
    ; on: the shape computes the gender, the shape is read over the referent, and the
    ; names disagree. Five digits, no branch here reaching a nothing.
    (defn word-gender-shape-check ()
        (add (add (add (add
            ; 1 — axiom-2: the word and the body in the sky are two cells, never one
            (wgd-bit (eq (wgd-same-cell? (wgd-mond) (wgd-the-moon)) 0) 1)
            ; 2 — axiom-3: the composition computes masculine for Mond
            (wgd-bit (eq (wgd-ask-gender (wgd-mond)) (wgd-masc)) 10))
            ; 3 — axiom-3: same composition, same gender
            (wgd-bit (eq (wgd-same-shape-same-gender?
                             (wgd-mond) (wgd-word "Kalendermond" "pgmc-n-stem-masc")) 1) 100))
            ; 4 — axiom-3: the shape read over the referent (Mädchen acks neuter)
            (wgd-bit (eq (wgd-shape-over-referent?) 1) 1000))
            ; 5 — names are 0..many: three names disagree, so the referent bore none
            (wgd-bit (eq (wgd-presupposition-holds?) 0) 10000)))
    0)
